WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.669 align:start position:0%
 
across southern europe  eyes are turning to the skies looking  for relief from a summer of scorching  fires  from italy where several regions are  ablaze  to the islands of greece enduring its  worst heat wave  in decades  in turkey residents use brushes and  buckets  to save their homes some are furious at  the government's lack of preparation  they burned us our lungs are burning  this is our future the future of our  children  the response is not enough it's  insufficient we need aerial support too  the state isn't helping without the  villagers it would be worse  there aren't enough firefighters planes  or helicopters  more aerial support is on the way from  the european union  volunteers have joined firefighters on  the front line  they've managed to extinguish most of  the fires on turkey's southern coast  where the flames have abated residents  are returning  to ruins  we are happy to have survived 10 to 15  of my trees have been burned  my properties are damaged the home i  live in is destroyed  there's nothing to do all my possessions  and money is gone  this year's fire season has been one of  the most destructive on record  according to eu data the extraordinary  summer  is testing the region's response to  increasingly extreme conditions  and we've got team coverage for you on  this with our dw correspondents on the  ground in turkey greece and  italy let's start with dw's yulia hahn  who joins us from  madagat one of the areas that has been  badly affected by the wildfires in  turkey julia what's the situation with  the fires there now  well the situation is devastating  firefighters here in the region continue  to battle the flames flames that have  left  nothing but destruction just look around  here  blackened tree trunks a grey landscape  and it looks like this  in many places here in the area we drove  around in our car last night  and we saw several forest areas burning  horrible images there was  thick yellow smoke which made it  difficult to breathe and now the turkish  authorities are trying to give the  impression it seems  that the crisis is under control to a  large extent but that is not what we're  witnessing  here on the ground according to data by  the european forest  information service there are three  times as many fires than usual this year  and more than one hundred thousand  hectares of forest land  have burned the eu julia has promised  turkey  water dropping planes and other forms of  assistance  has that helped arrived yet is it is it  making things better  well first of all the turkish government  has come under huge criticism for not  having been  well equipped enough to put out the  blazes when they erupted about  a week ago there were hardly any water  dropping  planes here in the country although this  region  is a region prone to wildfires this is  something  known now the number of firefighting  planes has increased  to 16 in total after help has arrived  here in turkey from  countries like russia and azerbaijan in  recent days a plane has also arrived  from croatia  yesterday and two more firefighting  planes are expected to arrive from spain  today  this has been as you mentioned organized  by the european union  5 000 firefighters some 5 000  firefighters are currently trying to get  the situation under control  but many people here in the area fear  that this won't be enough  julia thank you very much for now that  was our correspondent julia han  and monivat turkey  well temperatures are expected to peak  at 45 degrees celsius in some parts of  greece this week  the prime minister says the country is  experiencing its  worst heat wave since 1987.  greek authorities are advising  households and businesses to conserve  electricity especially during  afternoon and evening peak times  let's cross over to greece now uh  standing by is dw correspondent florian  schmitz in  thessaloniki florian it's been extremely  hot in greece how are people there  coping with it  the only way to cope with the heat is  actually to avoid it  of course people here in greece are used  to high temperatures especially  in july and august the peculiar the out  of the ordinary  is the duration of this heat wave  peaking around today with a temperatures  of about  46 degrees which is 114 degrees  fahrenheit the government advises people  to stay  inside drinks uh to drink lots of of  water  and to use the air conditioning not too  excessively though because  you must understand that greek buildings  many times are not well insulated i live  on the top floor of my building and my  apartment heats up to  cozy 38 degrees without my air  conditioning running 24 7. you can  imagine the drain on the already fragile  greek  power grid it's especially bad here in  the cities we don't have a lot of trees  a lot of  natural green so um the municipalities  keep buildings public buildings open for  people  who don't have access to climatized  housing but of course whoever can  escapes the cities to uh to go to the  beach and cool up there  so people are trying to stay cool to  what extent  is this extreme heat igniting a  political  discussion in greece florian  well for the time being people are very  busy with a dealing with the heat  basically but of course  we remember uh we see the the wildfires  and remember  remember the devastating fires in mati  in 2018  uh where more than 100 people lost their  lives so  especially environmental organizations  uh say that they've been warning  this and the previous governments to  take better precautions they say  we need better investments in heavy  machinery in  airplanes in in trucks and helicopters  uh we need better uh communication among  authorities  and we also need to invest more into the  maintenance of ford is  which means to um to pick up dry leaves  to to pick up  dry woods to uh prevent the fires  from spreading so quickly and also of  course to raise awareness to make people  um understand that barbecuing now  um and putting uh cigarette bumps in in  the forest can actually cost  lives florian thank you very much cw's  florian schmitz there in  thessaloniki  extreme weather has emergency services  on high alert in italy too  heavy rain and floods have hit the north  of the country  while wildfires are ravaging the south  the italian fire service says they've  carried out over 700  operations in the past 24 hours on  wildfires burning across the central  and southern parts of the country  let's bring in jacopo lentini here he's  a journalist  in palermo jacob where what is the  situation  like where you are in the south of the  country  first of all good morning thanks for  having me now  let me give you some number from the  15th of june until today  firefighter have responded to over 37  000 uh intervention uh  8 000 of which just in sicily which is  where i am i'm in palermo the main city  of  sicily uh now situation is looking a  little better than the weekend  where as you said uh there were about  800  um wildfires recorded all over the  country  now uh the city of catania um in the  eastern  region in the eastern of sicily has  shown some pretty bad images  uh the airport has been shut down for a  few hours over the weekend  the fire has rich bathing establishment  on the beach of catania and people were  forced to  evacuate so there's about  still um 11 or 10 11  active main wildfires sicilian  government has  requested the help of the national  government because it's unable to face  the issue with its own resources i'm  talking about about  30 30 to 40 additional  firefighters and civil protection  department squad  that have been sent from northern italy  regions  okay it sounds like it's a quite a  critical situation there  indeed uh jacopo lintini a journalist in  palermo  thank you very much  no words would you like me to provide  you with some more um  in-depth information thank you for now  thank you for now we'll come back to you  perhaps later thank you very much jacob  now let's try to put all this in context  we've got professor  moji latif a meteorologist at the  helmholtz center for ocean  research with this he's an author of  best-selling climate change  books including two hot times uh thank  you for being with us professor first of  all what do you make  of these extreme these multiple extreme  weather events that are happening  around the same time can you explain it  yeah i mean what we are seeing now is  that uh we are slowly leaving  the climatic comfort zone  um we have been in a relatively stable  climate for  many millennia however due to global  warming due to  anthropogenic so man-made global warming  we are  losing we are leaving this comfort zone  and  we are seeing extreme weather all around  the world  could be heat waves could be  extreme rainfall with  extreme flooding and you know there is a  kind of  unvisible hand and this is global  warming which  uh comes in different flavors and uh  yeah so we have warned uh for many years  uh that this can happen and now we are  seeing that it is happening  we've just been showing some images of  the fires have been raging  in southern europe of course there's  also been massive flooding  in europe and in china elsewhere as well  is  climate change happening faster  than expected  no not not really if we uh go  back 30 years ago when the first  simulations were made regarding the  influence of carbon dioxide  on the earth's climate then the climate  basically  followed what we have predicted uh  decades ago and so this is exactly what  we anticipated and we don't really see a  sign that we have  underestimated the climate  the climatic evolution it's right on  and unfortunately the political leaders  didn't really listen to our warnings and  this is now the consequences that we see  i wanted to ask you about that because  we're here in europe where the eu seems  to be trying to show some leadership in  tackling climate change but  this is a truly global challenge are the  eu  and governments around the world doing  what's necessary  to save the planet  no not really they put out  plans they define goals but they don't  act let me give you just one number  since 1990  the global carbon dioxide emissions  increased by 60 percent  six zero and uh  even this year 2021  we expect that carbon dioxide emissions  will rise again after the decline  last year due to the corona lockdown  and this shows that the  political leaders do not really  implement what is necessary and what is  necessary  our system systemic changes  okay so we have basically uh get away  worldwide from the fossil fuels  uh towards the renewable energies  and this is simply not happening fast  enough  and we have to work on this  professor latif thank you very much that  was professor moji blatif climate  scientist at the helmholtz center for  ocean research in  keel thank you you're welcome

